<title>CHILE: Codelco's Andina mine sees no drought impact.</title>
<headline>Codelco's Andina mine sees no drought impact.</headline>
<dateline>SANTIAGO 1996-11-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Chile's state copper company Codelco's Andina division said it does not expect to suffer any production difficulties as the result of a serious drought which is affecting central Chile.</p>
<p>&quot;We do not have any (water) problems which affect production now or in coming months,&quot; spokesman Antonio Varas told Reuters by telephone from the mine.</p>
<p>Officials at Codelco's El Teniente division and Exxon Minerals' Los Bronces mines have said their pits could suffer production problems as a result of the drought which is the worst in the area in 30 years.  </p>
<p>The Andina division has taken a series of measures to improve its water use including increasing the amount of water recycled in the flotation process, said Varas.</p>
<p>Andina, which lies north of Santiago, produces 150,000 tonnes of fine copper a year.</p>
<p>--Margaret Orgill, Santiago newsroom, 562-699-5595x212</p>
